  <div>
    <div class="workflow-title">Observable Feeding Pattern</div>
    <ol class="workflow-steps">
      <li><div><strong><span class="rx" data-len="───────────────">&nbsp;</span> Query</strong>Both heads query simultaneously. <span class="rx" data-len="──────────">&nbsp;</span> pulls ALPR movement patterns, The Equity Reaper court records, asset forfeiture case types. Target package assembled without warrant.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Third-Party Doctrine · IJIS Springboard]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Net Equity Assessment</strong><span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span> pre-seizure analysis commissioned. Title search. Appraisal. Net equity calculation. Properties meeting seizure threshold enter quarterly inspection rotation. <span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span> handbook §3.12.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Civil Asset Forfeiture — Preponderance Standard]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Quarterly Physical Inspection</strong><span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span> agents perform drive-by inspection of flagged real properties. Condition documented. Photographs taken. Pre-seizure file updated. Operational cover not required but preferred.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: HSI AIRG Handbook §4.5]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Operation Authorization</strong>Named operation authorized. Multi-agency task force assembled. Local agencies deputized. Operation provides cover for final property verification and accelerates timeline before owner liquidates.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Equitable Sharing · 28 CFR Part 9]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Coordinated Sweep</strong>72-hour arrest operation. Warranted individuals taken. Properties simultaneously accessed. DAG-71 forms filed. Assets bifurcate into<span class="rx" data-len="─────">&nbsp;</span>AFF pipeline (<span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span>) and <span class="rx" data-len="───">&nbsp;</span> pipeline (<span class="rx" data-len="───">&nbsp;</span>).<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Comprehensive Crime Control Act 1984]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Dual Fund Deposit</strong><span class="rx" data-len="───────">&nbsp;</span> receives <span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span>-custodied assets. <span class="rx" data-len="───">&nbsp;</span> receives <span class="rx" data-len="───">&nbsp;</span>-seized assets. Same operation. Same community. Two self-financing machines fed simultaneously.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: 28 USC §524(c) · 31 USC §9703]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong><span class="rx" data-len="─────────────────">&nbsp;</span> Distribution</strong>Local agencies receive percentage from both pipelines proportional to direct participation — work hours expended, qualitative contribution to the seizure. Federal machines retain remainder. Operation funds next operation. Hotlist alerts persist on all associated vehicles.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Equitable Sharing · 21 USC §881(e)(3) · "bears a reasonable relationship to the degree of direct participation"]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Probation Officer Deployment — Warrantless Entry</strong>Probation and parole officers are routinely present at mass arrest operations. Their operational value is precise: any individual on their caseload has signed a search waiver as a condition of supervision. That waiver extends to any premises where the probationer resides. The probation officer's presence converts a warrant requirement into a paperwork formality — or eliminates it entirely. The operation accesses addresses that would otherwise require judicial authorization. The probation department files a DAG-71 and receives equitable sharing proportional to the work hours contributed. The reward is monetary and documented in the machine's own guidance. Federal law states the share must reflect the agency's degree of direct participation in the law enforcement effort resulting in the forfeiture. Showing up with a search waiver is direct participation.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Equitable Sharing · DOJ Guide to Equitable Sharing · 21 USC §881(e)(3) · Standard Probation Search Waiver Conditions]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Surveillance Node Conversion — The Non-Asset Pipeline</strong>Individuals arrested without seizeable equity do not exit the machine as waste. They exit as permanent infrastructure. A plea bargain produces probation. Probation requires installation of a court-mandated supervision application deployed across tens of thousands of clients statewide. The application requires biometric enrollment: face, voice, location. It captures GPS at every check-in. It collects phone numbers of contacts and vehicle data. The vendor's own privacy policy confirms collection of app activity including in-app search history and video conferencing. Data is retained under government contract with potentially indefinite retention and unlimited government rights to use, dispose of, or disclose. The probationer's address is confirmed. Their associates are mapped. Their vehicle is in the ALPR network. Their household is covered by the search waiver. They are not byproduct. They are a node — enrolled, located, accessible without additional judicial process for the duration of supervision. The surveillance expansion is a direct output of the asset pipeline, not a separate system.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Standard Probation Conditions · Search Waiver Extension to Premises · Supervision App Privacy Policy · Enrolled client population: documented in vendor case studies]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Residual Predation Loop</strong>Operation concludes. ALPR alert profiles remain active on tagged vehicles. Pre-seizure files on remaining properties retained. Quarterly inspection rotation continues. Probation nodes remain enrolled. The calm before next harvest season.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: No documented off-switch]</span></div></li>
    </ol>
