    <div class="workflow-title">Observable Feeding Pattern</div>
    <ol class="workflow-steps">
      <li><div><strong>Household Data Aggregation</strong>Public health information systems aggregate household data across interconnected state services — benefits receipt, Medicaid enrollment, mental health treatment, substance abuse reporting, juvenile justice contact, school records, and housing assistance — into a unified case record. The Bad Faith Adjuster' HHS platform manages these workflows for state agencies across the country, providing incident detection, case tracking, triage, analysis, investigation, resolution, and trending. All available to authorized users. All connected through the same API infrastructure that connects courts, property records, and law enforcement.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Tyler HHS Platform · tylertech.com/solutions/health-human-services · Children First Software]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Vulnerability Score Assignment</strong>Predictive risk models assign a vulnerability score to each household based on weighted variables drawn from the aggregated record. Documented screening variables include: SNAP and cash benefits receipt, Supplemental Security Income, county health and mental health treatment, drug and alcohol abuse reporting, juvenile probation involvement, neighborhood poverty index, prior hotline contact within a two-year window, household associate history, and compliance scoring. The <span class="rx" data-len="─────────">&nbsp;</span> Family Screening Tool uses 131 such indicators. A score above 18 on a 20-point scale triggers mandatory action — no caseworker discretion permitted.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: AFST · Allegheny County PA · ACLU Family Surveillance by Algorithm · 2023]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Score Withheld from Court</strong>The risk score that triggered the case is intentionally withheld from the court to prevent the score from biasing the judge. The American Bar Association notes this creates a legally anomalous condition: a key piece of evidence affecting a case outcome cannot be examined by attorneys or the court. The score influences the investigation. The investigation produces the court case. The court case cannot see the score. The algorithm operates upstream of the legal process and outside its evidentiary rules.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: ABA Children's Rights · Winter 2024 · "intentionally withheld from court"]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Network Mapping and Associate Profiling</strong>Household associates identified through benefits co-recipients, shared addresses in housing records, co-defendants in court records via The Equity Reaper, and vehicle co-location data from <span class="rx" data-len="──────────">&nbsp;</span> ALPR. The network is scored alongside the primary household. Families are labeled risky by association. The ACM Journal on Responsible Computing documents how aggregate household-level risk scores amplify systemic discrimination through inclusion of variables that capture families' involvement with the criminal legal system or public benefits programs.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: ACM JRC · "Algorithmic Harms in Child Welfare" · 2023 · Families labeled risky by association]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Law Enforcement Coordination and Entry Selection</strong>Once a case is created and assigned, law enforcement coordination is determined by household record. If a search waiver is on file through parole or probation, entry does not require a warrant — the waiver travels with the record. If criminal drug history is present, a drug task force is assigned. If a history of violence appears in associate records, <span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span> is assigned. The coordination matrix routes the appropriate entry mechanism before the first contact occurs. The knock-and-talk visit is a calendar event, not a spontaneous decision.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Parole/Probation Search Waiver Conditions · USMS Deputation · DTF Equitable Sharing]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Probable Cause Construction</strong>The entry produces observations. Observations are documented. Documentation supports an affidavit. The affidavit supports a warrant if one is subsequently needed. The sequence reverses the traditional evidentiary order — the scoring system identifies the household, the entry confirms the predetermination, the documentation formalizes the conclusion. The Berkeley Public Policy Journal notes the algorithm models community and family court decisions rather than actual maltreatment — the system predicts its own future intervention, not an underlying harm.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: BPPJ · "Algorithms and Child Welfare" · Fall 2020 · "models community and family court decisions"]</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Case Outcome</strong>The household presents no seizable equity meeting the <span class="rx" data-len="────">&nbsp;</span> threshold. The case does not route to the asset forfeiture pipeline. It routes to the public health outcome pipeline. What moves through this beast is not currency, vehicles, or real property. The reader may observe what the scoring variables predict, what the mandatory threshold triggers, what the coordinated entry produces, and what the case outcome documents. The compendium does not name it. The system's own records do.<span class="doctrine-tag">[Doctrine: Known Unknown · Primary source documentation pending · Reader inference invited]</span></div></li>
    </ol>
